s question: Given only the data on P and Q, how do we know\nwhether we are estimating the demand function or the supply function? Al-\nternatively, if we think we are fitting a demand function, how do we guar-\nantee that it is, in fact, the demand function that we are estimating and not\nsomething else?\nA moment’s reflection will reveal that an answer to the preceding ques-\ntion is necessary before one proceeds to estimate the parameters of our\ndemand function. In this chapter we shall show how the identification\nproblem is resolved. We first introduce a few notations and definitions\nand then illustrate the identification problem with several examples. This\nis followed by the rules that may be used to find out whether an equation\nin a simultaneous-equation model is identified, that is, whether it is the re-\nlationship that we are actually estimating, be it the demand or supply func-\ntion or something else.\n19.1 NOTATIONS AND DEFINITIONS\nTo facilitate our discussion, we introduce the following notations and\ndefinitions.\n735\n\nGujarati: Basic IV. Simultaneous−Equation 19. The Identification © The McGraw−Hill\nEconometrics, Fourth Models Problem Companies, 2004\nEdition\n736 PARTFOUR: SIMULTANEOUS-EQUATION MODELS\nThe general M equations model in M endogenous, or jointly dependent,\nvariables may be written as Eq. (19.1.1):\nY = β Y +β Y +···+β Y\n1t 12 2t 13 3t 1M Mt\n+γ X +γ X +···+γ X +u\n11 1t 12 2t 1K Kt 1t\nY =β Y +β Y +···+β Y\n2t 21 1t 23 3t 2M Mt\n+γ X +γ X +···+γ X +u\n21 1t 22 2t 2K Kt 2t\nY =β Y +β Y +···+β Y\n3t 31 1t 32 2t 3M Mt\n+γ X +γ X +···+γ X +u\n31 1t 32 2t 3K Kt 3t\n................................................................\nY MT =β M1 Y 1t +β M2 Y 2t +···+β M,M−1 Y M−1,t\n+γ X +γ X +···+γ X +u\nM1 1t M2 2t MK Kt Mt\n(19.1.1)\nwhere Y ,Y ,...,Y = M endogenous, or jointly dependent, variables\n1 2 M\nX ,X ,...,X = Kpredetermined variables (one of these Xvariables\n1 2 K\nmay take a value of unity to allow for the intercept\nter